1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Two metres under a lake.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:04,880
A pier above, a clay-white shape below.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:06,480
One person.

4
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:08,160
Eyes open.

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:09,620
Mouth open.

6
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,220
In the corner of the frame, a stopwatch

7
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:12,740
starts.

8
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:14,540
Ten seconds.

9
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:16,020
Thirty.

10
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:17,420
A minute.

11
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,620
She is not holding her breath.

12
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:21,400
She is breathing.

13
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,540
The water goes in.

14
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:24,940
Something comes out.

15
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:28,200
Her chest rises and falls like anyone's on

16
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:28,640
dry land.

17
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:30,740
Nobody on the pier knows how.

18
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:32,700
The answer is not in her lungs.

19
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:34,340
It is in a number.

20
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:37,340
Two hundred and fifty.

21
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:38,940
This is the story of one rule.

22
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:40,940
A person who can breathe water.

23
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,000
Of a litre of air and a litre

24
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:43,420
of lake.

25
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:45,160
And the gap between them.

26
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,620
Of a fish that solves the problem with

27
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:48,220
a plumbing trick.

28
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,180
And a lung that was never built for

29
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:50,620
it.

30
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,440
Of mice in a tank in the nineteen-sixties,

31
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:56,280
and premature babies in a hospital in Philadelphia.

32
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:58,740
And an empty pier at dusk.

33
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:00,740
Could a human really do it?

34
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,220
And what would it cost her, minute by

35
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:03,540
minute?

36
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:06,300
This film is built on physiology textbooks,

37
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:10,140
diving manuals, clinical reviews and old laboratory reports.

38
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:12,780
It is a calculation, not a prediction.

39
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:14,440
And the unknowns are many.

40
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:17,540
Every number comes from a named source, or

41
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:18,560
from arithmetic we show.

42
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,120
Where the sources disagree, we say so.

43
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:22,540
Start with the number.

44
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:23,760
Then the water.

45
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:24,960
Then the fish.

46
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:26,180
Then the liquid.

47
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:28,700
First, what a body needs.

48
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,212
At rest, an adult uses about two hundred

49
00:01:31,212 --> 00:01:32,940
and fifty millilitres of oxygen a minute.

50
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,500
That is the figure in standard physiology teaching.

51
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:38,424
One medical exam-prep page gets there from three

52
00:01:38,424 --> 00:01:40,720
and a half millilitres per kilogram, for a

53
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:41,620
seventy-kilogram adult.

54
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:43,730
About two hundred and forty-five.

55
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:44,980
Two hundred and fifty.

56
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:45,560
Hold on to that.

57
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:47,600
In air, it is easy to supply.

58
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:49,515
A litre of dry air is about twenty

59
00:01:49,515 --> 00:01:51,880
point nine percent oxygen, according to the United

60
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:53,820
Nations food agency's fisheries manual.

61
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:55,860
That is about two hundred and nine millilitres

62
00:01:55,860 --> 00:01:57,300
in every litre.

63
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:59,680
Resting breathing moves about five to eight litres

64
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:01,260
of air a minute, according to the standard

65
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:02,600
reference on minute ventilation.

66
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:03,740
Call it six.

67
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:05,100
Six litres of air.

68
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:07,000
Two hundred and fifty millilitres of oxygen.

69
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,160
A comfortable, quiet job.

70
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:12,180
That is the deal the lungs were built

71
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:12,500
around.

72
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:13,900
Now change the fluid.

73
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:16,220
Water holds oxygen too.

74
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:18,120
Not much, says the sensor maker.

75
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:22,600
A manufacturer of dissolved-oxygen sensors says air-saturated water

76
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,540
at twenty-five degrees holds about eight point three

77
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:27,880
milligrams of oxygen per litre.

78
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:30,050
Convert that, and it is about five point

79
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:31,020
eight millilitres.

80
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:33,560
Our conversion, using a standard value for oxygen's

81
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:33,940
density.

82
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,940
Two hundred and nine in air.

83
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:37,620
Five point eight in water.

84
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:39,700
About thirty-six times less.

85
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:41,340
Colder water holds more.

86
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:42,640
Warm water holds less.

87
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,660
But the order of magnitude stays.

88
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:47,440
So how much water does she need?

89
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,285
If her lungs took every last molecule, two

90
00:02:50,285 --> 00:02:52,460
hundred and fifty divided by five point eight

91
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:54,040
is forty-three litres a minute.

92
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,560
That is the floor.

93
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:57,200
Physics will not let it go lower.

94
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:59,220
But lungs do not take every molecule.

95
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:02,160
An arXiv paper on lung ventilation in mammals

96
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:04,800
reports that exhaled air is sixteen to eighteen

97
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:06,940
percent oxygen, when the air going in is

98
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:10,660
twenty-one.

99
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,080
A drop of three to five points.

100
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:14,120
That is roughly a seventh to a quarter

101
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:14,860
of what comes in.

102
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:16,000
Our arithmetic.

103
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:17,740
Take the generous end.

104
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:18,320
A quarter.

105
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:21,900
Two hundred and fifty, divided by five point

106
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:23,740
eight, divided by a quarter.

107
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:26,180
One hundred and seventy-two litres a minute.

108
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,320
At the stingy end, it is more than

109
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,100
three hundred.

110
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,580
How much can a person breathe?

111
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,820
A physiology module at Monash University gives a

112
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:37,856
mean maximum voluntary ventilation of about a hundred

113
00:03:37,856 --> 00:03:39,620
and forty litres a minute for healthy young

114
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:39,840
men.

115
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,580
Range, a hundred to a hundred and eighty.

116
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:44,060
For women, about a hundred.

117
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,880
Seventy to a hundred and twenty.

118
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,840
That is a short test, the module says,

119
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,880
with the subject trying as hard as possible.

120
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:52,080
Her resting need is higher than that.

121
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:52,960
At rest.

122
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:55,800
Then there is what water is.

123
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:58,340
It is about eight hundred times denser than

124
00:03:58,340 --> 00:03:58,580
air.

125
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,095
Course notes give a ratio of about eight

126
00:04:01,095 --> 00:04:03,040
hundred and thirty, a flashcard set says.

127
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,900
One litre of water weighs one kilogram.

128
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:08,320
A hundred and seventy-two litres a minute is

129
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,740
a hundred and seventy-two kilograms.

130
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:11,060
Through the throat.

131
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:12,360
Every minute.

132
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:13,540
While resting.

133
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,240
And the lung has a second problem.

134
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,320
It breathes in and out through the same

135
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:18,600
pipes.

136
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:21,960
A textbook puts the anatomical dead space at

137
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,040
about a hundred and fifty millilitres, out of

138
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:25,960
a resting breath of five hundred.

139
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:28,300
That is the volume of the airways themselves.

140
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:30,360
Whatever sits there at the end of an

141
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,260
outbreath is stale.

142
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:33,240
The next breath mixes with it.

143
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:35,040
In air, that costs a little.

144
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:37,680
The textbook says it leaves the oxygen pressure

145
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:39,400
in the lung lower than in the air

146
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:39,840
outside.

147
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,980
In water, count it.

148
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:43,680
Five hundred millilitres in.

149
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,340
A hundred and fifty of it stale.

150
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:47,720
Three hundred and fifty reach the air sacs.

151
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,180
To move a hundred and seventy-two litres of

152
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:52,430
fresh water through them, that is nearly five

153
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:53,680
hundred breaths a minute.

154
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,620
Or take the biggest breath she has.

155
00:04:57,160 --> 00:04:59,700
Total lung capacity is about six litres, and

156
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,140
the usual vital capacity is four point six

157
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:04,100
to four point eight, depending on the source.

158
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:06,180
A full breath every time.

159
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:07,760
Nearly forty a minute.

160
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:10,060
Each one about four and a half kilograms

161
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:10,540
of lake.

162
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:11,720
Our arithmetic.

163
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:13,800
Neither is a pace a person holds.

164
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:16,000
No source we read says anyone has.

165
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,260
All this is hypothetical.

166
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:20,400
Here is what happens in the real world.

167
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,000
When someone inhales water,

168
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,000
a clinical update on drowning describes two paths.

169
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:27,560
In roughly ten to twenty percent of victims,

170
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:28,900
the throat clamps shut.

171
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:29,960
Laryngospasm.

172
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:33,360
The airway closes and the person asphyxiates, with

173
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:34,600
little water reaching the lungs.

174
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:37,580
Another source puts it lower, under ten to

175
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:38,880
fifteen.

176
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:41,500
In most, the throat relaxes and water goes

177
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:41,820
in.

178
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:45,320
Victims typically aspirate about two to four millilitres

179
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:47,640
per kilogram, according to the same reviews.

180
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:49,220
A small volume.

181
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:50,620
But it does damage.

182
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,080
The lungs line their air sacs with a

183
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:54,500
thin film called surfactant.

184
00:05:54,940 --> 00:05:55,700
It keeps them open.

185
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:57,400
Water disrupts it.

186
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:58,500
The sacs collapse.

187
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:00,640
Oxygen can no longer cross.

188
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:03,740
Older teaching said fresh water and salt water

189
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:04,940
injure the lungs differently.

190
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:07,800
Current clinical sources say the practical difference is

191
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:07,960
minor.

192
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:10,040
The result is the same chain.

193
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,740
Collapse, falling oxygen, rising carbon dioxide, and then

194
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:15,060
the heart.

195
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:18,120
So our hero's lungs would need two things.

196
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:19,940
A way to avoid the damage.

197
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:22,080
And a way to move far more water

198
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:23,220
than any lung has moved.

199
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:24,360
Remember the second.

200
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:26,640
Fish do it.

201
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:27,540
Every minute.

202
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:28,400
How?

203
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:29,480
Two tricks.

204
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:30,900
One.

205
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:32,820
Their gills run blood and water in opposite

206
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:33,380
directions.

207
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:36,440
The most oxygen-rich blood meets the most oxygen-rich

208
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:36,800
water.

209
00:06:37,280 --> 00:06:38,440
The least meets the least.

210
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:40,880
Diffusion keeps working along the whole surface.

211
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:42,320
How well does it work?

212
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:43,420
Sources differ.

213
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:45,880
A textbook says up to eighty-five percent of

214
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:46,820
the oxygen in the water.

215
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:49,020
A school resource says about eighty.

216
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:51,060
The Natural History Museum in London says up

217
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:52,100
to seventy.

218
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:54,560
An animal-physiology text says ninety or more is

219
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:54,900
possible.

220
00:06:55,420 --> 00:06:55,928
Take eighty.

221
00:06:55,928 --> 00:06:58,813
Two hundred and fifty, divided by five point

222
00:06:58,813 --> 00:07:00,620
eight, divided by point eight.

223
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:01,980
About fifty-four litres a minute.

224
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:05,036
Not one hundred and seventy-two.

225
00:07:05,036 --> 00:07:05,620
Two.

226
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:06,840
The water flows one way.

227
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,580
It goes in the mouth, over the gills,

228
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:09,220
out the side.

229
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:10,340
No dead space.

230
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:12,600
No stale pool in the airways.

231
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:15,520
A physiology text says fish greatly reduce the

232
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:17,760
energy cost of moving water by keeping that

233
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:19,520
flow continuous and in one direction.

234
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:21,960
A lung does the opposite.

235
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:23,880
It is a dead-end sac.

236
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:25,580
The same pipe in, the same pipe out.

237
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:27,600
So there is a first way to make

238
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:28,320
her breathing work.

239
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:29,360
Change her.

240
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:30,480
Give her gills.

241
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:32,380
Or something with gills' efficiency.

242
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:33,640
A one-way flow.

243
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:35,080
A far better extraction.

244
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:36,940
That is a different animal.

245
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:39,240
Nothing in this film says it is impossible.

246
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:41,000
It is not a lung anymore.

247
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,720
Whether any human body could be rebuilt that

248
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:44,860
way is not known.

249
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:48,700
Duke University, early nineteen-sixties.

250
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:51,600
According to a Gizmodo history, Johannes Kylstra's team

251
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:54,380
trained mice to breathe saline loaded with oxygen

252
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:55,940
under a pressure of a hundred and sixty

253
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:56,660
atmospheres.

254
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:58,380
The mice died minutes later.

255
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:00,500
Carbon dioxide had built up.

256
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:03,380
Another account says rodents and even dogs lasted

257
00:08:03,380 --> 00:08:04,320
up to about an hour.

258
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:06,020
Accounts differ on the time.

259
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:07,700
They agree on the cause.

260
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:10,000
The saline delivered enough oxygen.

261
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:12,440
It did not clear the carbon dioxide fast

262
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:12,760
enough.

263
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:16,700
The same fisheries manual says carbon dioxide is

264
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:19,180
far more soluble in water than oxygen is.

265
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:21,320
Which is one reason gills shed it easily.

266
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:24,220
But a saline-filled lung is not a gill.

267
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:25,340
Our reading:

268
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:27,900
it is the same dead-end sac, filled with

269
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:29,140
a liquid that barely moves.

270
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:31,220
The sources do not test that.

271
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:34,100
Mid-nineteen-sixties.

272
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:35,320
Clark and Gollan.

273
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:36,180
They switched the liquid.

274
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:37,000
Not water.

275
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:38,820
A perfluorocarbon.

276
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:41,100
A fluid that dissolves both gases.

277
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:44,120
Gizmodo says their mice breathed it at normal

278
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:44,620
pressure.

279
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:46,840
Rats stayed under for up to twenty hours.

280
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:48,160
Cats, for weeks.

281
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:51,620
An older encyclopedia mirror adds one detail that

282
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:51,980
matters.

283
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:54,800
The colder the liquid, the lower the animal's

284
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:55,320
breathing rate.

